course: dictionary
lesson: 1985
title: Comprehension: understanding what you read
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Comprehension means understanding. When you understand what you read or hear, you have good comprehension. «Decoding (turning letters into words) and comprehension (understanding what you read) are two different skills.» You can read every word aloud and still not understand the story.
explain: A reading comprehension is also a school exercise: you read a short text, then answer questions about it. Some answers are written straight in the text, and some you must work out for yourself. Read the text again slowly, and check each answer against what the text actually says.
explain: Something beyond comprehension is so strange or huge that you cannot understand it. Tricky questions can catch you out, because the answer may not be what you expect. Read exactly what the text says, not what you think it should say.
fact: Comprehension means understanding, or knowing what something means.
fact: A comprehension is a school reading exercise where you read a text and answer questions about it.
fact: «Roots ending in d, de, se or nd usually take -sion: comprehend, comprehension; expand, expansion; tense, tension.»
fact: Listening comprehension is used in language lessons, where you understand what you hear.
fact: Something beyond comprehension is too strange or huge to understand.
exercise: «Decoding (turning letters into words) and comprehension (understanding what you read) are two different skills.» What does comprehension mean here? | Reading out loud very fast | Copying the text neatly | Turning letters into words | *Understanding what you read
exercise: Which of these is a comprehension exercise? | *A short text followed by questions about it | A list of spelling words to copy out | A song with a chorus | A map of the school
exercise: Which word is spelled with -sion, from the root comprehend? | *comprehension | comprehensive | comprehend | comprehended
test: «Tricky comprehension questions test whether you read exactly what the text says, not what you expect it to say.» What do tricky questions test? | *Whether you read exactly what the text says | How fast you can run | Your favourite colour | Whether you like the story
test: Something that is beyond comprehension is | Something you already know well | Easy to explain | *Too strange or huge to understand | Very cheap to buy
exam: Which sentence uses comprehension to mean understanding? | The comprehension rang the bell at the end of the day | Ravi ate his comprehension for lunch | *Ravi's comprehension of the story was excellent, so he knew exactly what had happened | Ravi's comprehension bag was bright red
